# Design — Optimize archive phase9 git calls

## Direction recap

Introduce a per-invocation accumulator that caches idempotent git queries within a single `finalizeRelease` call. Thread it through subfunctions. Explicit invalidation on every state mutation. Public API stays backward-compatible.

## Type design

### Avoid name collision

Existing `GitFinalizeContext` (line 2791) is the **static input contract** for `finalizeRelease` (changeId, workdir, archiveMode, autoPush, skipPush, artifactPaths, expectedMainCheckout). Do NOT rename or reuse — it stays as-is.

New accumulator type: **`FinalizeInvocationState`**
- "Invocation" → per-call lifecycle (constructed at entry, discarded at return)
- "State" → mutable cache + invalidation tracking
- "Finalize" → ties to `finalizeRelease`

### Shape

```ts
// Internal type — not exported. Lives in git-finalize.ts.
interface FinalizeInvocationState {
  // Static — populated at construction, never changes
  readonly mainCheckout: string;
  readonly defaultBranch: string;
  readonly deps: GitFinalizeDeps;

  // Cached idempotent queries (lazy)
  remoteUrl?: string;
  route?: FinalizationRoute;
  originHeadSha?: string;
  localHeadSha?: string;
  committerIdent?: { ok: boolean; message?: string };
  mainInProgress?: { inProgress: boolean; state?: string };

  // Fetch dedup flag
  originDefaultFetched: boolean;
}

// Factory — constructs at finalizeRelease entry
function createState(
  mainCheckout: string,
  defaultBranch: string,
  deps: GitFinalizeDeps,
): FinalizeInvocationState {
  return {
    mainCheckout,
    defaultBranch,
    deps,
    originDefaultFetched: false,
  };
}
```

### Cache accessors (one per cached fact)

```ts
function getRoute(state: FinalizeInvocationState): FinalizationRoute {
  if (state.route) return state.route;
  state.route = classifyFinalizationRoute(state.mainCheckout, state.defaultBranch, state.deps);
  return state.route;
}

function getOriginHeadSha(state: FinalizeInvocationState): string {
  if (state.originHeadSha) return state.originHeadSha;
  const result = runGitOrThrow(state.mainCheckout, ["rev-parse", `origin/${state.defaultBranch}`], state.deps);
  state.originHeadSha = result.trim();
  return state.originHeadSha;
}

// (similar for remoteUrl, localHeadSha, committerIdent, mainInProgress)
```

### Fetch dedup

```ts
function ensureOriginDefaultFetched(state: FinalizeInvocationState): RunGitResult {
  if (state.originDefaultFetched) {
    return { status: 0, stdout: "", stderr: "" };
  }
  const runGit = state.deps.runGit ?? defaultRunGit;
  const result = runGit(state.mainCheckout, ["fetch", "origin", state.defaultBranch]);
  if (result.status === 0) {
    state.originDefaultFetched = true;
  }
  return result;
}
```

**Key invariant:** `originDefaultFetched` flips to `true` only on successful fetch. A failed fetch is NOT cached as success — caller can retry.

## Invalidation matrix (C4 — release-critical)

This is the most safety-critical surface. Every mutation × every cached entry:

| Mutation | remoteUrl | route | originHeadSha | localHeadSha | committerIdent | mainInProgress | fetched |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `commitArchiveArtifacts` (commit on workdir, not main) | keep | keep | keep | keep | keep | keep | keep |
| `commitDirtyMainCheckpoint` (commit on main) | keep | keep | keep | **invalidate** | keep | **invalidate** | keep |
| `mergeChangeBranch` (merge to main) | keep | keep | **invalidate** | **invalidate** | keep | **invalidate** | keep |
| `pushToOrigin` (push main → origin HEAD moves) | keep | keep | **invalidate** | keep | keep | keep | keep |
| `pushChangeBranch` (push change branch — main unaffected) | keep | keep | keep | keep | keep | keep | keep |
| `resetMainToOriginDefault` (`git reset --hard origin/<default>`) | keep | keep | **invalidate** | **invalidate** | keep | **invalidate** | **invalidate** |
| `executePullRequestHandoff` (push + fetch) | keep | keep | **invalidate** | **invalidate** | keep | keep | **invalidate** |

### Why each cell

- **`remoteUrl`** never invalidated — `origin` remote URL does not change during one invocation.
- **`route`** never invalidated — derived from `remoteUrl` + branch-protection rules (gh API). Both stable during one invocation. The 3 existing call sites (2862 pre-merge, 3029 post-merge, 3067 push-failed) all read the same route; the route does not depend on local branch state.
- **`originHeadSha`** invalidated by any mutation that moves origin's default branch tip (push of main) or any operation that fetches (which can update origin's known SHA): `mergeChangeBranch`, `pushToOrigin`, `resetMainToOriginDefault`, `executePullRequestHandoff`.
- **`localHeadSha`** invalidated by any mutation that moves local default branch tip or working state: `commitDirtyMainCheckpoint`, `mergeChangeBranch`, `resetMainToOriginDefault`, `executePullRequestHandoff`.
- **`committerIdent`** never invalidated — git identity is `.git/config`-based; mid-invocation edits are not a realistic concern.
- **`mainInProgress`** invalidated by any mutation that could change whether a merge/rebase/cherry-pick/revert is in progress: `commitDirtyMainCheckpoint` (completes a checkpoint commit), `mergeChangeBranch`, `resetMainToOriginDefault`. NOT invalidated by `executePullRequestHandoff` — that operation pushes/fetches but does not start or complete an in-progress merge.
- **`fetched`** invalidated only by `resetMainToOriginDefault` and `executePullRequestHandoff` — operations that explicitly re-fetch as part of their semantics (so the next `ensureOriginDefaultFetched` will fetch again, ensuring the local view is current after a reset/handoff).

### Invalidation API

```ts
type MutationKind =
  | "commit-archive-artifacts"
  | "commit-dirty-main-checkpoint"
  | "merge-change-branch"
  | "push-to-origin"
  | "push-change-branch"
  | "reset-main-to-origin-default"
  | "execute-pull-request-handoff";

function invalidate(state: FinalizeInvocationState, kind: MutationKind): void {
  switch (kind) {
    case "commit-archive-artifacts":
    case "push-change-branch":
      // No cache entries affected
      return;
    case "commit-dirty-main-checkpoint":
      delete state.localHeadSha;
      delete state.mainInProgress;
      return;
    case "merge-change-branch":
      delete state.originHeadSha;
      delete state.localHeadSha;
      delete state.mainInProgress;
      return;
    case "push-to-origin":
      delete state.originHeadSha;
      return;
    case "reset-main-to-origin-default":
    case "execute-pull-request-handoff":
      delete state.originHeadSha;
      delete state.localHeadSha;
      delete state.mainInProgress;
      state.originDefaultFetched = false;
      return;
  }
}
```

### Failure-rollback invariant (AC4)

Every mutation calls `invalidate(state, kind)` **before** returning (whether success or throw). Pattern:

```ts
function commitDirtyMainCheckpoint(
  mainCheckout: string,
  changeId: string,
  deps: GitFinalizeDeps,
  state?: FinalizeInvocationState,
): { committed: boolean; commitSha?: string; error?: string } {
  try {
    // ... existing work ...
    state && invalidate(state, "commit-dirty-main-checkpoint");
    return { committed: true, commitSha };
  } catch (err) {
    state && invalidate(state, "commit-dirty-main-checkpoint");
    throw err;
  }
}
```

`invalidate` runs in finally / both paths so a thrown mutation never leaves stale cache that retry logic would trust.

## Public API evolution (backward compat — C1, AC5, AC6)

### Signature strategy

Existing signatures gain an **optional `state?: FinalizeInvocationState` trailing parameter**. External callers (none in production; 9 test sites) continue to work with `state === undefined` → current behavior (no caching).

```ts
// Before
export function classifyFinalizationRoute(
  mainCheckout: string,
  defaultBranch: string,
  deps: GitFinalizeDeps = {},
): FinalizationRoute

// After (no signature change to classifyFinalizationRoute itself — it remains
// the underlying uncached call. Caching is layered above it via getRoute().)
```

Public entrypoint `finalizeRelease` signature unchanged:

```ts
export async function finalizeRelease(
  ctx: GitFinalizeContext,
  deps: GitFinalizeDeps = {},
): Promise<GitFinalizeOutcome> {
  // NEW: internally construct state and propagate to subfunctions
  // ... existing body, but subfunction calls now pass `state` ...
}
```

### Internal subfunction evolution

Internal subfunctions that today call `classifyFinalizationRoute(mainCheckout, defaultBranch, deps)` directly gain an optional `state` parameter:

```ts
// Before
function verifyRemoteNotAhead(
  mainCheckout: string,
  defaultBranch: string,
  deps: GitFinalizeDeps = {},
): { ok: boolean; reason?: string }

// After
function verifyRemoteNotAhead(
  mainCheckout: string,
  defaultBranch: string,
  deps: GitFinalizeDeps = {},
  state?: FinalizeInvocationState,  // ← trailing optional
): { ok: boolean; reason?: string }
```

Inside the body, `state ? getRoute(state) : classifyFinalizationRoute(mainCheckout, defaultBranch, deps)` etc.

### Production call site (AC6)

`plugin/src/tools/change.ts:3322` requires **no source change**. The optimization is internal to `finalizeRelease` and its subfunctions. The site continues to call:

```ts
await finalizeRelease({
  changeId,
  workdir: worktreePath,
  expectedMainCheckout: store.paths.root,
  archiveMode,
  autoPush,
  artifactPaths: ...,
})
```

### Test call sites

9 test sites in `git-finalize.test.ts` continue to work unchanged (no `state` argument → no caching → current behavior). New tests will pass `state` directly to verify cache hit/miss/invalidation.

## Test strategy (AC7)

### New test infrastructure

Add to `git-finalize.test.ts`:

```ts
/** Mock runGit that records every call. */
function makeRecordingRunGit(impl: (cwd: string, args: string[]) => RunGitResult): {
  runGit: GitFinalizeDeps["runGit"];
  calls: Array<{ cwd: string; args: string[] }>;
} {
  const calls: Array<{ cwd: string; args: string[] }> = [];
  return {
    runGit: (cwd, args) => {
      calls.push({ cwd, args });
      return impl(cwd, args);
    },
    calls,
  };
}
```

### Required new test cases (AC7)

1. **Cache hit on repeated query** — construct state, call `getRoute(state)` twice, assert `classifyFinalizationRoute` invoked once (via recordingRunGit counting `remote get-url origin` calls).
2. **Cache miss on first query** — fresh state, call `getRoute(state)`, assert exactly one underlying call.
3. **Invalidation per mutation kind** — parameterized test over the matrix; for each `(mutation, cacheKey)` cell marked **invalidate**, populate cache, run mutation, assert cache cleared.
4. **Failure-rollback invalidation** — for each mutation, simulate thrown git op, assert cache cleared even on throw.
5. **Fetch dedup happy path** — call `ensureOriginDefaultFetched(state)` twice, assert underlying `fetch` invoked once.
6. **Fetch dedup on failure** — first `fetch` returns non-zero, assert `originDefaultFetched` remains false (next call retries).
7. **End-to-end finalizeRelease cache effectiveness** — full `finalizeRelease` run on no-op fixture with recordingRunGit; assert fetch invoked ≤1 time and total git calls ≤12 (validates SC1, SC2).

### Existing test preservation (AC5)

All 9 existing `finalizeRelease` test sites continue to construct `ctx` without `state` → no caching → behavior identical. CI must remain green without modifying these tests.

## Bench strategy (UD1 — warn-only CI)

Add `git-finalize.bench.test.ts` (separate file to keep unit-test surface clean):

- Uses the same no-op fixture as the end-to-end cache test
- Times `finalizeRelease` invocation with `performance.now()`
- Compares against a baseline constant (e.g. `const NO_OP_BASELINE_MS = 1500`)
- On exceedance: `console.warn` with measured ms + baseline (never throws)
- Tagged with `it.skip` by default; opt-in via `BENCH=1` env var
- Wired through `bin/oc-test` smoke tier (already runs in CI; bench is warn-only so it doesn't fail)

Baseline will be calibrated post-implementation by running on the reference machine and recording the measured value as the new baseline.

## Implementation strategy (for prep)

### Step ordering (task graph hint)

1. Add `FinalizeInvocationState` type + `createState` + `invalidate` + `MutationKind` enum + accessors (`getRoute`, `getOriginHeadSha`, etc.) — additive, no behavior change yet.
2. Add `ensureOriginDefaultFetched` — additive, not wired in.
3. Add `state?: FinalizeInvocationState` trailing parameter to internal subfunctions — additive, defaults to current behavior.
4. Wire `state` through `finalizeRelease` body — construct state at entry, pass to all subfunction calls. Behavior unchanged when state is propagated but no mutations fire.
5. Wire invalidation calls at each mutation site (using the matrix above).
6. Convert existing redundant `classifyFinalizationRoute` calls (2862, 3029, 3067) to `getRoute(state)` — first observable optimization.
7. Convert existing redundant `fetch origin <defaultBranch>` calls to `ensureOriginDefaultFetched(state)`.
8. Add new tests (AC7 test cases).
9. Add bench test file.
10. Verify SC1, SC2, SC3 against new tests + bench.

### Risk hotspots

- **Step 5 (invalidation calls)**: every mutation must call invalidate correctly. Missed invalidation = release bug. adv-reviewer at acceptance MUST verify the matrix implementation matches this design doc.
- **Step 6-7 (call conversion)**: mechanical but error-prone — easy to convert one call site and miss another that should also be converted. P25 related-scan after conversion.

## Cross-cutting concerns

### Concurrency

`FinalizeInvocationState` is per-invocation, single-threaded. `finalizeRelease` is `async` but does not await between mutations and queries in a way that would expose the state to race. The existing code is fully synchronous between git calls (the only `await` is at the entry dispatch in `change.ts`); internal subfunctions are sync. No race surface introduced.

### Worktree-aware caching

`mainCheckout` is part of state's static identity. Cache entries are scoped to that main checkout. If a test constructs state with one mainCheckout and then mutates a different path, the cache would be wrong — but tests don't do this (production code doesn't either). The `mainCheckout` is `readonly` in state to make this invariant visible.

### Failure modes introduced

- **Cache-entry-not-invalidated**: the central risk. Mitigation: matrix tests + adv-reviewer.
- **State propagates across invocations**: impossible — state is local to `finalizeRelease`, discarded at return.
- **Cache returns stale value across mutation**: covered by matrix tests.

## Alternatives considered (LBP)

### A1: Memoize at module level (singleton)

Rejected — would leak cache across invocations and across test runs. Per-invocation state is correct scope.

### A2: Async memoization with WeakMap keyed by `(cwd, defaultBranch)`

Rejected — overkill. Adds complexity for no observable gain; finalize is single-shot per call.

### A3: Replace redundant queries with explicit upfront "preflight" gathering all facts once

Tempting but restructures the flow — explicitly out of scope per DONT1. Saves more calls but risks regression in the sequential invariant logic.

### A4: Add caching layer to `makeBoundedRunGit`

Rejected — wrong layer. `makeBoundedRunGit` wraps every git call with timeout/cancel; cache would be a leaky abstraction (some git calls are safe to cache, some are not). The accumulator pattern at the subfunction level is the right granularity.

## Open questions for prep

- Should `ensureOriginDefaultFetched` re-fetch on a long-running invocation (>30s elapsed since first fetch)? Probably no — adds complexity for marginal benefit; the existing code does not do this. Document as a follow-up if it becomes relevant.
- Should we expose the invalidation matrix as a static test fixture (JSON) so tests + reviewers can mechanically verify completeness? Likely yes — adds auditability. Defer decision to prep.
